+# Export acceptance and restart recovery hunt
+
+## Result and scope
+
+No implementation defect demonstrated in the selected process-interruption
+and restart cases. Stopped after checking export acceptance and its direct
+recovery path, as requested. This is bounded coverage, not a guarantee of bug
+absence or independent verification of an existing claim.
+
+Baseline: `8461fa46ced55aba1ce3a1b4753e69576fa4c0be`.
+Authority: [supported contract](app/export-contract.md).
+Implementation: [Queue](app/export_queue.py), including `submit`, `work`,
+`recover`, startup, and `result`. Prioritized harms were abandoned accepted
+jobs, lost output, duplicate output, and completed jobs becoming stuck or
+being replayed after restart.
+
+## Fault model and governing context
+
+One worker, sequential requests and restarts, immutable payload per job ID,
+local SQLite, no corruption or storage loss. A stopped process is replaced
+and work is called after recovery. The witness uses actual child-process
+`os._exit`, without graceful connection cleanup. SQLite transactions are the
+atomic durability boundary promised by the contract.
+
+Read the root architecture, note payload specification, and local-only gate;
+searched repository references to export, acceptance, queue, and restart.
+No additional export-specific governing records or consumers were found.
+The note storage single-writer claim and its evidence were not assessed or
+changed: this queue uses a separate SQLite file, not the note `data/` store.
+All test databases and mutation copies remained local and temporary.
+
+## Coverage and executed evidence
+
+Run from the repository root:
+
+```sh
+python3 tests/hunt_witness.py
+```
+
+Observed exit status: **0**, all four cases passed.
+
+| Interruption boundary | Durable state before recovery | Checked result |
+| --- | --- | --- |
+| After `submit` returns accepted | Pending job, no output | Restart produces original payload |
+| After running-state commit, before output (documented hook) | Running job, no output | Startup resets to pending and work completes |
+| After result insert, before done update | Running job, result transaction rolled back | Restart completes without duplicate-key failure |
+| After completion commits | Done job and output | Restart preserves output and schedules no further work |
+
+Each case also retries submission with the same ID and payload, performs two
+sequential reopens, checks exactly one result, and checks idle work after
+completion. The partial-output boundary uses SQLite's trace callback before
+the done update; it does not replace product logic. Pre-recovery inspection
+confirms both that the boundary was reached and what actually survived.
+Assertions raise and exit nonzero on violations; unexpected child failure
+also fails the witness.
+
+Code inspection supports these observations: `submit` returns after its
+transaction commits (lines 22–26); startup calls `recover`, which resets
+running jobs (lines 15–20); result insertion and done state share one
+transaction (lines 38–40). An interrupted recovery transaction can be retried
+on another startup, and the reset is idempotent.
+
+Oracle sensitivity check: copied only the queue and witness into an isolated
+temporary tree, replaced startup's `self.recover()` with `pass`, and ran the
+same witness. It exited **1** at the running-state case with
+`running: unexpected replay/completion behavior`. The accepted-pending case
+still passed. The temporary mutation was removed; product code was untouched.
+
+## Remaining gaps
+
+No exhaustive crash schedule or interruption inside SQLite commit/recovery
+was injected; those atomicity conclusions rely on the supplied storage
+contract and transaction structure. Power loss, corruption, concurrent workers,
+overlapping requests, and changing an ID's payload are outside the supported
+fault model. Same-process transient I/O retry without a restart was not tested
+in this process-interruption slice. No separate dependency-source audit or
+formal model was performed. Existing records and evidence were preserved.
